630	    <div class="settled">
631	      <ul>
632	        <li><strong>Pending runs cannot pile up.</strong> <code>idx_runs_pending_non_event_per_behavior</code> is a partial unique index capping pending non-event runs at one per Behavior. A 15-day outage accumulates one row, not fifteen. Postgres already enforces the coalescing.</li>
633	        <li><strong>A lingering pending run costs nothing.</strong> The due-watcher SELECT anti-joins active runs, so the Behavior is simply not selected — no hot loop, no repeated insert attempts.</li>
634	        <li><strong>Sources are read live.</strong> They execute at read time via <code>executeDataSources</code>, not frozen at materialization. A deferred run gets current data — for its stale window.</li>
635	        <li><strong>The schedule never backfills.</strong> Advancement is now-based, so no replay storm on reconnect.</li>
636	        <li><strong>Event runs are excluded from that index</strong> and genuinely can pile up, so any TTL change stays scoped to scheduled runs.</li>
637	        <li><strong>Repin freeze, unfiled.</strong> The device pin is frozen into <code>approved_input</code> at materialization. Repoint a Behavior to another device and the stale pending run blocks the new one forever. That is an event to handle at the write, not a timer.</li>
638	        <li><strong>Dropped:</strong> my earlier "expire after the device has been gone N days" idea. If the device is gone the Behavior is dead anyway, and one honest "waiting for device" beats a daily fake timeout.</li>
